Let stored secrets survive a restart.

BaseSecretStorageService keeps secrets in memory unless the encryption service
reports itself available, and the browser implementation always answers false,
so on this platform every extension sign-in is discarded when the app is closed
and has to be repeated on the next launch.

The answer is not a claim that anything is encrypted. encrypt() and decrypt() in
this service are the identity function, and secrets sit in IndexedDB as plaintext
either way; what changes is whether they are kept at all. The protection is the
Android application sandbox: the database belongs to this app and no other app
can read it, which on this platform is the same boundary a keychain entry sits
behind. The comment in the source says so plainly rather than leaving a future
reader to assume the name means what it usually means.

Worth noting this only became worth doing once the server port stopped moving:
IndexedDB is keyed by origin, so before the port was pinned these secrets would
have been unreachable after every cold start regardless.

